\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{One sign link changes two plaquettes}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{On the cellular #{2\times2} torus take
#{G=C_2=\{1,-1\}} acting on #{F=\mathbb Z}. Set every positive
labeled link to #{1} except #{U_1(0,0)=-1}. The two path
products in \ref{fdip-00SL} give plaquette holonomy #{-1} at
#{(0,0)} and #{(0,1)}, and #{1} at #{(1,0)} and #{(1,1)}.}

\p{For any Abelian link assignment on a periodic two-torus, the
product of all plaquette holonomies is #{1}: each labeled link
occurs once and its inverse occurs once. A single negative
plaquette is therefore impossible in this sign model. The
coefficient representation over #{\mathbb Z} detects both
negative plaquettes, so #{\extd_U^2\ne0}.}

\p{These are labeled cellular edges, including both positive
edges between a pair of vertices. The period-one exception for
the loop-free finite-set calculus in \ref{fdip-00O0} is a different
model and is unchanged.}
